[{:seon.task/name "report-beyond-its-pulls"
  :seon.task/plan "`seon.problems/report` (`my.root/problems`) is linear in recorded problems and flat in clean tasks, as designed, but each problem set (one signature, one red run, one dead run) costs ~570 KB and ~550 us against ~400 KB and ~330 us priced from its pulls and EDN reads (`seon.cost/units` :db/pull, :db/pull-attr, :edn/read-char; Jolt 00a3b321). Either the read does work beyond its pulls (find it with seon.profile: the :fns of one report call, and `seon.cost/measure` per step) or the estimate misses a term (then derive it and say what work it is). Done when seon.problems-test/problems-reads-only-the-problems and problems-cost-nothing-for-a-clean-program are green with their :overrun keys deleted."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.problems-test/problems-reads-only-the-problems seon.problems-test/problems-cost-nothing-for-a-clean-program}}
 {:seon.task/name "an-observers-defect-is-a-problem-not-invalid-input"
  :seon.task/plan "Still true (re-read 2026-10-23 for step E, its citations brought current): `my.root/problems` answers `(seon.problems/health @(:conn sys) (seon.root/errors sys))`. ```seon.root/errors``` answers the api's fold (`:seon.error/fold`), or, when the api could not answer, its defect (a `:seon.error/base` map: the root task seon.root/a-proc-answers-every-request-it-took); ```seon.problems/health``` and ```seon.problems/report``` take only a fold, so on that defect the problems read itself is refused as invalid input and root sees no problems at all, exactly when something is wrong. Correct: they take the observer's answer as it is: a fold counts as today; a defect is reported as a problem row of its class (an error map is data, AGENTS §4), beside main's own rows, which are counted from main alone. The proof: the report of a db with one recorded error and the defect {:seon.error/type :seon.root/no-answer :seon.error/operation 'seon.root/errors :seon.error/message \"the api did not answer\"} (a whole :seon.error/base map) as the observer's answer holds the recorded error's row and a row naming the defect."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.problems-test/an-observers-defect-is-a-problem-row}}]
